1
00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:04.559
There's so many things that make these AI agents a truly incredible technology,

2
00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:09.680
but one of those is the ability for them to self-heal and to get better over time.

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.560
What means that these agents need to have memory? They need to learn what they're doing right,

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.920
what they're doing wrong, what they should do different in the future.

5
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.239
So in this lesson, we're going to talk about everything related to memory,

6
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:27.040
how they share knowledge with each other, what are the different places that memory can come from.

7
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.159
It's going to be a very exciting lesson. It's going to unlock a lot of potential

8
00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:33.200
for your own use cases. So let's dive right in.

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.360
In CrewAI and most agent implementations, you will find a few different types of memory.

10
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.599
But in CrewAI, there's three specific types of memory that are super important.

11
00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:45.040
Short-term memory, long-term memory, and entity memory.

12
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.040
Short-term memory is meant to help agents exchange information beyond the added context.

13
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.360
And what that means is that agents, as they go through their tasks and actions,

14
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.119
they will write in that memory and allow other agents to read off that.

15
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:04.160
Long-term memory, on the other side, is meant to help these agents self-reflect

16
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.199
to understand the ways for improving over time. So as these agents get done with their tasks,

17
00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:13.120
they will compare the actual output with the expected output,

18
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.279
extract learnings, and store data for later usage. So when they run the same tasks again,

19
00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:23.519
they can understand what are the things that they did well and they could do more of,

20
00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:28.639
or things that they should do less of. And you also have entity memory that helps

21
00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:34.800
these agents remember things, recognizable companies, products, processes, and etc.,

22
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.120
along with their descriptions. An example of what an entity memory

23
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.199
might be encompassed of is this idea of persons, organizations, and locations.

24
00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:50.879
So it starts to learn all these different things and store them so it doesn't need to infer or

25
00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:55.839
learn what they are later down the road. If you want to enable memory for your agents,

26
00:01:55.839 --> 00:02:00.559
all you've got to do is turn one flag on, and that is the memory flag, to true

27
00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:06.480
under your crew instance. And that will allow all your agents to start to use these memories.

28
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.399
And the way that it works is that if we go back into our ReAct loop, you can actually see that

29
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:18.800
now all those three different databases for long, short, and entity memory are going to be feeding

30
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.600
information into the thought process so that the agents know more about not only what can do better,

31
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.759
but the things that it's talking about and things that other agents might be doing.

32
00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:35.759
And then once the agent's done with the answer, a new step's added at the end that basically

33
00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:42.080
add the reflections that can inject memory back into those databases that are going to be used

34
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.839
later. Not only that, but you also have a way to generate memories for the agents

35
00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:53.600
yourself. And the way that you can do it is by using CrewAI training. And CrewAI training

36
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.639
is something that you run in our terminal that will, at the end of the execution, prompt you

37
00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:04.399
to give the agent feedback. And that feedback will be used then to generate memories that are

38
00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:10.000
going to be injected back into those databases. And now that will again be injected back into

39
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.360
the thought process as this agent runs in the future. So in the end of the day, the cool thing about

40
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:22.399
memory is that there's two types or two ways of you generating them. One is by just going through

41
00:03:22.399 --> 00:03:28.720
the motions of executions and that getting injected into those memory databases. And the other one is

42
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.000
training with the human feedback, where you are providing clear guidance on what are the things

43
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.320
that the agents are doing right and what are the things that agents are doing wrong. And that is

44
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.199
then generating memories. At some point, especially as you play around with memories, you might find

45
00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:48.320
yourself trying to understand how you reset those memories. Well, the good thing is all you got to

46
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.839
do is run this one single command called CrewAI reset memories, and that will automatically clear

47
00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:59.039
them up for you. And you can start recreating them from the get go, either by executing these agents

48
00:03:59.039 --> 00:04:04.800
or providing them with feedback. Now, whether you're generating memories or training with feedback,

49
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:11.199
the additional context can have measurable impact into the agent performance because it's going to

50
00:04:11.199 --> 00:04:17.920
help controlling things like behavior or format or style. So if during a feedback session you tell

51
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.880
your agents that you want the answers to always be markdown, it's actually going to create a

52
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.320
memory about that. And now that instruction is going to be automatically added to every agent

53
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:35.679
execution in that way, forcing a specific format. Or if you want it to be super funny about it,

54
00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:41.200
that will create a memory that will then be injected, forcing a specific style. So you can

55
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:48.079
see here how memory can actually be a very strong and important way for you to control more about

56
00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:53.760
how your agents work and how they get work done. Now, memory is not the only way for you controlling

57
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.760
your agents. With memory, you understand that the data gets selectively added to the context during

58
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.839
the runtime, and it gets updated by either human feedback or by having an internal LLM as a judge.

59
00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:12.559
And then you have knowledge. And knowledge is data that also gets selectively added,

60
00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:18.799
but it's pre-added to your agent. What it means is that that is not dynamically generated.

61
00:05:18.799 --> 00:05:24.799
So if you have a specific source of data, something that you want your agent to be aware of

62
00:05:24.799 --> 00:05:30.320
as it goes about running these tasks, you can preload that knowledge into the agent,

63
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.640
and that will be selectively used as the agent goes through the execution.

64
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:42.799
So these sources of knowledge can be, for example, text files, either raw text or PDF documents,

65
00:05:42.799 --> 00:05:48.959
or might also be structured data like CSV files or Excel spreadsheets or even JSON documents.

66
00:05:49.519 --> 00:05:53.839
In the end of the day, feeding this data into your agents is extremely easy.

67
00:05:53.839 --> 00:05:59.760
All you got to do is load this content, and then depending on the sort of knowledge, the sort of

68
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.959
type you have for knowledge, you will use different classes to embed that into your agent.

69
00:06:05.519 --> 00:06:10.880
And from that point on, you can now make sure that your agent has those sources within it,

70
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.760
meaning that during the ReAct loop, the agent now will be able to grab selective pieces of

71
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.839
information out of that knowledge in order to give you a proper answer.

72
00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:27.440
And that is an extremely helpful way for you to include extra information into your agent

73
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.359
that you want to make sure that is persisted throughout any execution that it does in the future.

74
00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:38.640
So again, knowledge is a different kind of memory. It's kind of like a special kind of memory

75
00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.079
that is basically being fed directly by you, the user. And the idea is that between memory

76
00:06:44.079 --> 00:06:48.720
and knowledge, it gives you a way to control these agents to enforce certain patterns.

77
00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.119
And you can do this either by having them improve themselves over time or by you giving

78
00:06:53.119 --> 00:06:59.359
feedback to them or providing and pre-feeding them with this knowledge prior executions.

79
00:06:59.359 --> 00:07:03.200
All those options work. Now, memory is extremely interesting,

80
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.119
but I'm very interested in what we're going to talk about in the next lesson,

81
00:07:07.119 --> 00:07:12.559
and that is guardrails. Guardrails give you the possibility to do more deterministic controls

82
00:07:12.559 --> 00:07:18.079
than memory and knowledge. So I'm very excited about this. We're going to dive into guardrails,

83
00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:22.480
how they work and how they can help you. So I'm going to see you in there in a second.

